{"type":"message","id":"a51f270b","parentId":"977b6fb2","timestamp":"2026-05-20T08:20:31.415Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_bLSaUxMnYkz8BxCk6ol2EAV9","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"set -e\nls -l /tmp/crypto-feature.jpg /tmp/chart.png /tmp/crypto-with-image.md /tmp/crypto-article.docx 2>/dev/null || true\nprintf '\\n--- symlink targets / realpaths ---\\n'\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport os\nfor p in ['/tmp/crypto-feature.jpg','/tmp/chart.png','/tmp/crypto-with-image.md','/tmp/crypto-article.docx']:\n    print(p, 'exists=', os.path.exists(p), 'islink=', os.path.islink(p), 'realpath=', os.path.realpath(p) if os.path.exists(p) or os.path.islink(p) else 'N/A', 'size=', os.path.getsize(os.path.realpath(p)) if (os.path.exists(p) or os.path.islink(p)) and os.path.exists(os.path.realpath(p)) else 'N/A')\nPY\nprintf '\\n--- first 20 lines of crypto-with-image.md ---\\n'\nsed -n '1,20p' /tmp/crypto-with-image.md || true","workdir":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-orchestrator","yieldMs":10000}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":1372,"output":639,"cacheRead":25216,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":27227,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779265220093,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DhWSBAvWiInxqUXr6mO2yKOmqxWWr"}}
{"type":"message","id":"d6f6350d","parentId":"a51f270b","timestamp":"2026-05-20T08:20:31.473Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_bLSaUxMnYkz8BxCk6ol2EAV9","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"lrwxrwxrwx 1 bhard bhard    41 May 20 07:43 /tmp/chart.png -> /tmp/crypto-run-20260520-074303/chart.png\n-rw-r--r-- 1 bhard bhard 67459 May 20 07:51 /tmp/crypto-article.docx\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 bhard bhard    43 May 20 07:43 /tmp/crypto-feature.jpg -> /tmp/crypto-run-20260520-074303/feature.jpg\n-rw-r--r-- 1 bhard bhard  8703 May 20 07:51 /tmp/crypto-with-image.md\n\n--- symlink targets / realpaths ---\n/tmp/crypto-feature.jpg exists= True islink= True realpath= /tmp/crypto-run-20260520-074303/feature.jpg size= 1076796\n/tmp/chart.png exists= True islink= True realpath= /tmp/crypto-run-20260520-074303/chart.png size= 62779\n/tmp/crypto-with-image.md exists= True islink= False realpath= /tmp/crypto-with-image.md size= 8703\n/tmp/crypto-article.docx exists= True islink= False realpath= /tmp/crypto-article.docx size= 67459\n\n--- first 20 lines of crypto-with-image.md ---\nMETA\nSEO Title: Canada crypto ATM ban | Coinography fraud impact\nMeta Description: See what Canada's proposed crypto ATM ban means for fraud risk, Bitcoin access, and regulation. Get the key numbers and signals now.\nURL Slug: canada-crypto-atm-ban\nCategories: Bitcoin News, Latest News\nPrimary Keyword: Canada crypto ATM ban\nSecondary Keywords: crypto ATM fraud, FINTRAC powers, Canada Bitcoin ATM market\n\n# Canada crypto ATM ban targets 10.1% of global kiosks\n\nCanada crypto ATM ban headlines hit the market after Ottawa proposed removing standalone crypto kiosks nationwide, a move aimed at a country that still hosts 10.1% of the world's machines. The proposal landed in the Spring Economic Update 2026 and framed these kiosks as a major channel for scams and money laundering.\n\nThe reaction matters because Canada is not a fringe market for this business. It was the first country to host a public Bitcoin ATM in 2013, and the phaseout would hit a visible retail access point in malls, gas stations, and convenience stores. For Bitcoin, this is less about price action and more about how regulators are redrawing the retail on-ramp map.\n\nThis is not a ban on owning crypto. It is a targeted move against a payment rail that officials now see as too easy to abuse.\n\n## Crypto ATM fraud: What It Means for Retail Access\n\nOttawa's proposal is narrowly aimed at standalone machines, not the broader ability to buy digital assets in person. [CoinDesk reported](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/04/29/canada-proposes-ban-on-bitcoin-atms-as-fraud-cases-mount) that officials described crypto ATMs as a primary method used by scammers to defraud victims and by criminals to place illicit cash into the crypto system. That language is important because it tells traders and operators where enforcement pressure is moving."}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":42,"aggregated":"lrwxrwxrwx 1 bhard bhard    41 May 20 07:43 /tmp/chart.png -> /tmp/crypto-run-20260520-074303/chart.png\n-rw-r--r-- 1 bhard bhard 67459 May 20 07:51 /tmp/crypto-article.docx\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 bhard bhard    43 May 20 07:43 /tmp/crypto-feature.jpg -> /tmp/crypto-run-20260520-074303/feature.jpg\n-rw-r--r-- 1 bhard bhard  8703 May 20 07:51 /tmp/crypto-with-image.md\n\n--- symlink targets / realpaths ---\n/tmp/crypto-feature.jpg exists= True islink= True realpath= /tmp/crypto-run-20260520-074303/feature.jpg size= 1076796\n/tmp/chart.png exists= True islink= True realpath= /tmp/crypto-run-20260520-074303/chart.png size= 62779\n/tmp/crypto-with-image.md exists= True islink= False realpath= /tmp/crypto-with-image.md size= 8703\n/tmp/crypto-article.docx exists= True islink= False realpath= /tmp/crypto-article.docx size= 67459\n\n--- first 20 lines of crypto-with-image.md ---\nMETA\nSEO Title: Canada crypto ATM ban | Coinography fraud impact\nMeta Description: See what Canada's proposed crypto ATM ban means for fraud risk, Bitcoin access, and regulation. Get the key numbers and signals now.\nURL Slug: canada-crypto-atm-ban\nCategories: Bitcoin News, Latest News\nPrimary Keyword: Canada crypto ATM ban\nSecondary Keywords: crypto ATM fraud, FINTRAC powers, Canada Bitcoin ATM market\n\n# Canada crypto ATM ban targets 10.1% of global kiosks\n\nCanada crypto ATM ban headlines hit the market after Ottawa proposed removing standalone crypto kiosks nationwide, a move aimed at a country that still hosts 10.1% of the world's machines. The proposal landed in the Spring Economic Update 2026 and framed these kiosks as a major channel for scams and money laundering.\n\nThe reaction matters because Canada is not a fringe market for this business. It was the first country to host a public Bitcoin ATM in 2013, and the phaseout would hit a visible retail access point in malls, gas stations, and convenience stores. For Bitcoin, this is less about price action and more about how regulators are redrawing the retail on-ramp map.\n\nThis is not a ban on owning crypto. It is a targeted move against a payment rail that officials now see as too easy to abuse.\n\n## Crypto ATM fraud: What It Means for Retail Access\n\nOttawa's proposal is narrowly aimed at standalone machines, not the broader ability to buy digital assets in person. [CoinDesk reported](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/04/29/canada-proposes-ban-on-bitcoin-atms-as-fraud-cases-mount) that officials described crypto ATMs as a primary method used by scammers to defraud victims and by criminals to place illicit cash into the crypto system. That language is important because it tells traders and operators where enforcement pressure is moving.","cwd":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-orchestrator"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1779265231472}}
